%I A400547 #9 Oct 05 2026 18:40:59
%S A400547 1,0,0,11760,14303068,40881867584
%N A400547 Number of partitions of the n X n grid into n edge-connected regions such that there is exactly one way to place n stars, one in each row, column and region, with no two stars in adjacent cells, not even diagonally adjacent ones.
%C A400547 The regions are unlabeled, and partitions that differ by a rotation or reflection are counted separately.
%C A400547 a(n) is the number of n X n puzzles with a unique solution of the one-star Star Battle type, also called Parks. Star Battle was invented by Hans Eendebak for the 2003 World Puzzle Championship (see Snyder 2013 link).
%C A400547 This sequence was motivated by LinkedIn's daily game "Queens", introduced in 2024, which made the one-star Star Battle puzzle widely popular (see Chen and Snyder 2024 links). There the stars are called queens, although, unlike chess queens (see A000170), they do not attack along diagonals: two stars may lie on a common diagonal if they are not adjacent.
%C A400547 In the terminology of Minevich et al., a(n) is the number of 1-tree n X n Parks puzzles with contiguous parks that have a unique solution. They found a(4) = 11760, proved that deciding whether an arbitrary 1-tree Parks puzzle has any solution is NP-complete even when the parks are contiguous, and remarked that no fast algorithm was known for counting puzzles with contiguous parks.
%C A400547 In a solution the star of row i is in column p(i), where p is a permutation with |p(i+1) - p(i)| >= 2 for 1 <= i < n (see A002464), and the n star cells lie in n different regions. Since A002464(2) = A002464(3) = 0, a(2) = a(3) = 0.
%C A400547 a(n) > 0 for n >= 1 except n = 2 and 3. For n >= 4, a permutation p as above is obtained by listing the even columns in increasing order, followed by the odd columns in increasing order. Given any such p and a row i, make each cell (j, p(j)) with j != i a region of its own, and let the remaining cells form one region. This region is edge-connected: no two removed cells touch, and the cells surrounding each removed cell within the board form an edge-connected set, so any path through a removed cell can be rerouted around it. Every solution uses the n-1 singleton regions, so the star of row i must be at (i, p(i)). Different pairs (p, i) give different partitions, since the singleton regions determine the missing row and column. This proves a(n) >= n*A002464(n) for n >= 4; the bound also holds for n = 1, 2, 3.
%C A400547 A partition with a unique solution is fixed by a symmetry of the square only if its solution is. For n >= 2 no solution is fixed by the reflection in a horizontal or vertical axis (this would require p(n+1-i) = p(i) or p(i) = n+1-p(i) for all i). Hence the reflection in a vertical axis pairs up the partitions, and a(n) is even for n >= 2.
%C A400547 The number of all partitions of the n X n grid into n edge-connected regions is A395493(n); a(n)/A395493(n) is approximately 0.187, 0.198, 0.115 for n = 4, 5, 6.
%C A400547 The numbers of partitions with at least one solution are 1, 0, 0, 13073, 23823556, 121562318341 for n = 1..6.
%C A400547 Up to rotation and reflection, the numbers of partitions with a unique solution are 1, 0, 0, 1479, 1788082, 5110241363 for n = 1..6, and the numbers of partitions with at least one solution are 1, 0, 0, 1662, 2979019, 15195368209 for n = 1..6.
%C A400547 No partition has more than A002464(n) solutions; the partition into rows and the partition into columns have exactly that many, and for n = 5 and 6 they are the only ones. For n = 6 the second largest number of solutions is 78, attained by 10 partitions.
%C A400547 Laszlo and Mukherjee count the placements without regions for the two-star version of Star Battle (A389766).
%H A400547 Ruiqi Chen, <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/news/story/linkedin-news-introduces-games-6715874/">LinkedIn News introduces games</a>, LinkedIn News, Apr 30 2024.
